{{#extend "layout" showSource=true}}

  {{#content "main"}}
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Recipe &middot; Handlebars</p>
    <h1>One Handlebars environment per instance</h1>
    <p>
      v2 creates the environment with <code>Handlebars.create()</code>, so the global
      <code>handlebars</code> module knows nothing about this site's helpers or partials.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="grid">
    <article class="card">
      <h3>The v1 idiom</h3>
      <p>
        A project helper that did <code>require('handlebars').partials[name]</code>. Under v2
        that object is empty: the helper returns an empty string, the page renders, the build
        is green, and the block is simply missing from the output.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="card">
      <h3>The v2 form</h3>
      <p>
        Register on <code>kiss.handlebars</code> and read the partials off the same
        environment. This site registers a <code>renderPartial</code> helper that way; the card
        beside this one is that helper rendering a partial by name.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="card">
      <h3>Rendered by the helper</h3>
      {{renderPartial "stock-note" model}}
      <p class="meta tone-good">If the helper had read the global module, this card would be empty and nothing would have said so.</p>
    </article>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <pre><code>kiss.handlebars.registerHelper('renderPartial', function (name, context, options) {
  const partial = kiss.handlebars.partials[name]
  if (!partial) return ''
  const template =
    typeof partial === 'function' ? partial : kiss.handlebars.compile(partial)
  // Pass the helper's own data frame through — that frame is how the page is
  // recorded as using the partial, so a save re-renders just this page.
  return new kiss.handlebars.SafeString(template(context, { data: options?.data }))
})</code></pre>
    <p class="meta">
      Handlebars' own <code>&#123;&#123;&gt; partial&#125;&#125;</code> needs no helper at all.
      This one exists for the v1 case it replaces: a partial chosen at run time and rendered
      against a context the template does not have. Pass <code>options.data</code> through as
      the recipe does: without it the partial still renders, but under <code>watch()</code> this
      page is not re-rendered when that partial changes.
    </p>
  </div>
  {{/content}}

  {{#content "demo"}}
  <p>
    The same rule covers helpers you register yourself, the six kiss ships, and every partial
    under <code>folders.partials</code> and <code>folders.layouts</code>. If code outside the
    instance needs one of them, hand it <code>kiss.handlebars</code> rather than importing the
    module — that is the whole of the migration.
  </p>
  {{/content}}

{{/extend}}
